e muscle tissue itself screaming as it's asked to contract to stiffen the trunk, pulling on surgical scars and inflamed tissue. Think of it this way: your hamstring muscle attaches to your pelvis, and a torn hamstring is a real injury. But you'd never say you have "pelvis damage." You have a muscle injury. The pelvis is fine, it’s just the anchor point. Similarly, a gunshot wound to the abdominal muscles is a muscle injury; the spine is just the anchor point. The injury is no more "spine-related damage" than a torn hamstring is "pelvis damage." So, to loop back to your original question: they need PT to walk again not because the leg is damaged, and not because the spine is damaged, but because the muscular system that connects the two—the core—has been directly wounded, surgically repaired, deconditioned, and made painful. That's a separate category, not just a fancier way of saying "spine damage."
